\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Three-layer architecture of MosaicOpt. The \emph  {application} layer holds the fusion policy as SQL \texttt  {ORDER BY} and \texttt  {LIMIT}. The \emph  {logical algebra} is the substrate contract: a small set of operators (including the new $\mu $ and $\Gamma $) over which the equational theory is stated. The \emph  {physical algebra} realizes each logical operator through concrete access paths. HNSW ($H_{V,q,\tau }$) bridges the logical vector selection $\sigma _{\lVert V-q\rVert <\tau }$ to a physical implementation whose cost depends on approximation parameters $ef$, $M$.}}{2}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:threelayer}{{1}{2}{Three-layer architecture of MosaicOpt. The \emph {application} layer holds the fusion policy as SQL \texttt {ORDER BY} and \texttt {LIMIT}. The \emph {logical algebra} is the substrate contract: a small set of operators (including the new $\mu $ and $\Gamma $) over which the equational theory is stated. The \emph {physical algebra} realizes each logical operator through concrete access paths. HNSW ($H_{V,q,\tau }$) bridges the logical vector selection $\sigma _{\lVert V-q\rVert <\tau }$ to a physical implementation whose cost depends on approximation parameters $ef$, $M$}{figure.caption.4}{}}
