\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Multi-path recall (a) versus the MosaicOpt algebraic approach (b). In (a) four independent channels produce ranked candidate lists and application-level score fusion decides the final ranking --- there is no equivalence relation over plans, so all optimization is heuristic. In (b) the user writes a single logical plan over the extended algebra; the RBO enumerates equivalent rewrites under the equational theory, and the CBO picks a physical realization. The fusion policy is explicit in the \texttt  {ORDER BY} expression; the substrate has no opinion on the weights.}}{4}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:multipath}{{2}{4}{Multi-path recall (a) versus the MosaicOpt algebraic approach (b). In (a) four independent channels produce ranked candidate lists and application-level score fusion decides the final ranking --- there is no equivalence relation over plans, so all optimization is heuristic. In (b) the user writes a single logical plan over the extended algebra; the RBO enumerates equivalent rewrites under the equational theory, and the CBO picks a physical realization. The fusion policy is explicit in the \texttt {ORDER BY} expression; the substrate has no opinion on the weights}{figure.caption.5}{}}
